$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>The crime and the trial outcome</h2>
<p>The occurrence was a night-time house robbery of singular violence: a gold chain snatched, resistance answered with a grinding stone, a knife wound to the hand, and bite marks on the shoulder and back. The trial court (S.C. No. 75 of 2017, arising from Crime No. 646/2015 of Kulithalai police station) convicted the appellant under Sections 449 and 394 IPC and imposed ten years&rsquo; rigorous imprisonment with fine on each count. The appeal attacked both the investigation and the identification.</p>

<h2>The appellate court&rsquo;s method</h2>
<p>The judgment separates two questions that defence arguments often merge: whether the investigation was defective, and whether the charge was proved. On the first, the Court was unsparing — the arrest and recovery evidence suffered serious defects, and the police version could not be treated as independently reliable. On the second, it turned to the evidence that did not depend on the police: the injured complainant (PW-1), whose presence at the scene was written on her body in medically documented injuries, and the witness who apprehended the appellant (PW-3). Their testimony, corroborated by the medical record, carried the case: <em>&ldquo;The prosecution has therefore proved the charges beyond reasonable doubt.&rdquo;</em></p>
<p>This is orthodox criminal appellate method. An injured eyewitness occupies a special evidentiary position — the injuries guarantee presence — and a defective investigation benefits the accused only where the remaining evidence cannot stand on its own.</p>

<h2>The sentence recalibration</h2>
<p>Having confirmed guilt, the Court revisited proportionality. Ten years on each count gave way to three: <em>&ldquo;a term of three years of Rigorous Imprisonment for each offence would meet the ends of justice,&rdquo;</em> the sentences concurrent and the period already undergone set off under Section 428 CrPC. Notably, the Court refused to count pending or acquitted cases as antecedents — a point of recurring importance, since charge-sheets without convictions are not criminal history. The bail bond was cancelled with a direction to surrender within a week.</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Guilt and sentence are separate appellate inquiries.</strong> Confirming a conviction does not freeze the trial court&rsquo;s sentence; proportionality is re-examined on the full record.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Injured witnesses anchor prosecutions.</strong> Their account, medically corroborated, can survive even a discredited investigation.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Defects matter where they create doubt.</strong> Flaws in arrest and recovery weaken the case only to the extent the conviction rests on them.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Antecedents mean convictions.</strong> Pending cases and acquittals cannot aggravate a sentence.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">Appeals under Section 374(2) CrPC — now Section 415 of the BNSS, 2023 for post-July 2024 proceedings — permit a full re-appreciation of evidence and sentence. Each outcome turns on the specific record; the reduction here reflects this Court&rsquo;s assessment of this crime and this offender, not a tariff for robbery cases.</div>
